#~
HTTP/3 "must refuse" tests -- the assertions the other HTTP/3 tests do not make.

Two things the client must REFUSE rather than quietly accept:
  1. a connection that is not actually HTTP/3 (silent downgrade)
  2. a caller-supplied content type carrying CR/LF (header injection)

Lives in programs/tests/ and runs from the NON-GATING network step, not the
gating regression suite. Two reasons, both learned the hard way:

  * The gating runners (run_regression.sh/.cmd) decide PASS/FAIL from the
    process EXIT CODE and never grep output, so a printed "FAIL" there is
    inert. The network step DOES grep for FAIL, so the assertion below
    actually bites.
  * The negative case deliberately points at a host that will never speak
    HTTP/3. On the ngtcp2 backend that means h3_run_loop burns its full
    30-second cap, and the gating runner's per-test timeout is 60s -- two
    such waits would kill the test and redden the build for a network
    condition. Exactly what this test must never do.

What it asserts: a host that does NOT speak HTTP/3 must FAIL rather than
quietly succeed over HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1. That is a real bug on any platform
and cannot be caused by network conditions. It matters most on Windows, where
WinHTTP treats HTTP/3 as a *preference* and falls back transparently -- so the
backend asserts WINHTTP_OPTION_HTTP_PROTOCOL_USED. An Http3Client that quietly
spoke HTTP/2 would be the same false promise this feature was fixing.

Gating on runtime.feature.http3 is why that property exists: the trap handlers
compile unconditionally, so without it this test could not tell "this build has
no HTTP/3 engine" from "the request failed".
~#
use Web.HTTP;
use System;

class Http3DowngradeTest {
	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		if(System.Runtime->GetProperty("runtime.feature.http3")->Equals("0")) {
			"SKIP: HTTP/3 not compiled into this build"->PrintLine();
			return;
		};

		pass := true;

		# --- positive: a real HTTP/3 host round-trips ----------------------
		# Never fatal: an outage or a blocked UDP 443 must not fail the build.
		client := Http3Client->New("cloudflare-quic.com");
		if(client->IsConnected() = false) {
			"  SKIP: could not establish an HTTP/3 connection (network?)"->PrintLine();
		}
		else {
			resp := client->Get("/");
			if(resp = Nil) {
				"  SKIP: HTTP/3 GET failed (network?)"->PrintLine();
			}
			else {
				code := resp->GetCode();
				body := resp->GetContent();
				size := 0;
				if(body <> Nil) { size := body->Size(); };
				"  info: GET status={$code} bytes={$size}"->PrintLine();
			};
		};
		client->Close();

		# --- negative: no silent downgrade (the real assertion) ------------
		# example.com serves HTTP/1.1 only. Reaching it and reporting success
		# means the client accepted a non-HTTP/3 connection -- a genuine bug,
		# not a network condition, so this one IS fatal.
		plain := Http3Client->New("example.com");
		if(plain->IsConnected()) {
			down := plain->Get("/");
			if(down <> Nil) {
				dc := down->GetCode();
				pass := false;
				"  FAIL: non-HTTP/3 host silently succeeded (status={$dc})"->PrintLine();
			}
			else {
				"  info: non-HTTP/3 host correctly refused"->PrintLine();
			};
		}
		else {
			"  info: non-HTTP/3 host refused at connect"->PrintLine();
		};
		plain->Close();

		# --- header injection: CR/LF in a caller-supplied content type ----
		# WinHttpAddRequestHeaders takes a CRLF-SEPARATED BLOCK, so a CR/LF in
		# a value is the API's own separator -- it appends a header line rather
		# than failing to parse. This was accepted (status 200) until the
		# validator landed. The ngtcp2 path transmits the raw octets, which a
		# downstream h3->HTTP/1.1 gateway can re-emit as a split.
		inj := Http3Client->New("cloudflare-quic.com");
		if(inj->IsConnected()) {
			payload := "x"->ToByteArray();
			evil := inj->Post("/", payload, "text/plain
X-Injected: pwned");
			if(evil <> Nil) {
				ec := evil->GetCode();
				pass := false;
				"  FAIL: CR/LF content type accepted (status={$ec}) -- header injection"->PrintLine();
			}
			else {
				"  info: CR/LF content type correctly refused"->PrintLine();
			};
		}
		else {
			"  SKIP: no HTTP/3 connection for the injection check"->PrintLine();
		};
		inj->Close();

		if(pass) {
			"PASS: HTTP/3 downgrade test"->PrintLine();
		}
		else {
			"FAIL: HTTP/3 downgrade test"->PrintLine();
		};
	}
}
